WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.633
Container selection is a security and maintainability decision, not only a performance detail.

2
00:00:04.633 --> 00:00:12.473
The container communicates how data is owned, how it grows, how callers access it, and what kinds of invalidation reviewers should expect.

3
00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:16.393
std::vector is often the right default for dynamic contiguous storage.

4
00:00:16.393 --> 00:00:20.313
It has simple ownership, cache-friendly iteration, and clear size behavior.

5
00:00:20.313 --> 00:00:27.440
It also reallocates as it grows, so pointers, references, and iterators into the vector can become invalid after some operations.

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.073
std::array is useful when the size is fixed and known by design.

7
00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:40.982
The fixed size can make bounds and lifetime easier to review. std::deque may fit designs that need growth at both ends or different stability

8
00:00:40.982 --> 00:00:44.189
properties, but its own iterator rules still need attention.

9
00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:51.673
Associative containers such as maps and sets can make lookup rules explicit, while unordered containers can make key-based lookup efficient.

10
00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:56.662
Their safety depends on correct key design, comparator behavior, hashing, equality, and resource limits.

11
00:00:56.662 --> 00:00:58.444
Element ownership should be deliberate.

12
00:00:58.444 --> 00:01:00.938
Storing values is often simpler and clearer.

13
00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:07.353
Storing unique_ptr, shared_ptr, or other owning objects can be appropriate, but it changes lifetime, sharing, and review questions.

14
00:01:07.353 --> 00:01:13.055
A container of raw owning pointers is usually a sign that ownership needs a clearer type.

15
00:01:13.055 --> 00:01:16.618
Growth and memory pressure should be part of the contract.

16
00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:24.102
If input can influence item count, reserve behavior, or element size, the code needs limits before storage grows without a boundary.

17
00:01:24.102 --> 00:01:29.447
Choose the container that makes the expected access pattern and lifetime easiest to reason about.

18
00:01:29.447 --> 00:01:35.862
The safest choice is often the one that turns design assumptions into visible code rather than hidden convention.

19
00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:38.000
Container choice also affects API clarity.

20
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.920
A function that accepts a span communicates a contiguous borrowed range.

21
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.553
A function that accepts a vector by value communicates ownership transfer or copying.

22
00:01:46.553 --> 00:01:50.116
A function that accepts a map communicates lookup by key.

23
00:01:50.116 --> 00:01:52.255
These choices help reviewers understand intent.

24
00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:56.887
When the container choice feels awkward, it may reveal a deeper design question.

25
00:01:56.887 --> 00:02:05.084
Perhaps the data should be streamed instead of stored, capped instead of unbounded, or converted into a narrower domain type before entering shared

26
00:02:05.084 --> 00:02:05.440
state.
